# database/ holds one-time dataset-prep artifacts (raw CSVs/xlsx, import scripts) never
# read by the running app — search_exercise_db reads from MongoDB (tools/database.py),
# not these files. Only exercises_dataset/media/ (the GIFs api/main.py mounts at
# /media/exercises) is actually needed at runtime. Excluding the rest cuts ~300MB from
# the build context, including a 281MB CSV that was stalling context transfer.
#
# The raw media/ directory itself (2648 individual files) is ALSO excluded here, even
# though it's needed — Docker Desktop's Windows-to-Linux-VM file-sharing layer is
# dramatically slower (to the point of hanging) transferring many small files versus
# one large one, independent of total byte size. media.tar (a single pre-built archive,
# see Dockerfile) is copied and extracted inside the container instead.
database/*
!database/exercises_dataset
database/exercises_dataset/*
!database/exercises_dataset/media.tar

# Qdrant's local-mode lock file — a runtime artifact recreated automatically on startup,
# never something to bake into an image. Also a real hazard at build time: while any
# process (e.g. a running dev server that triggered the RAG pipeline) holds Qdrant open,
# this file is OS-level locked ("Device or resource busy"), and Docker's build-context
# reader can hang indefinitely trying to read a locked file rather than failing fast —
# confirmed as the actual root cause of repeated "load build context" stalls this session.
rag_data/qdrant/.lock
